## Intent & Decisions
Let the provider configure the prefill pool of a prefill/decode (PD)
deployment, which the inference API already supports.

Settled choices:
- An optional pd attribute holds prefill_resources and
  prefill_autoscaling (min, max, capacity_classes, concurrency), mapped
  to the API DeploymentPD message. The existing resources and
  autoscaling attributes keep configuring the decode pool.
- A change to pd updates the deployment in place. hot_load uses
  RequiresReplace because the API treats it as immutable; pd is not.
- Create and update both send Pd, because UpdateDeployment replaces the
  whole specification. Without Pd in the update request, any update
  from Terraform would remove the prefill pool.
- Superseded: the first version left out prefill capacity_classes to
  save 39 lines. A /code-review high pass showed the cost: every
  Terraform update cleared prefill classes set outside Terraform with
  no visible diff, and the docs did not say the prefill pool ignores
  autoscaling.capacity_classes. The user asked to fix the review
  findings, so the attribute is back. The schema, request mapping, and
  null-preserving read are shared with the decode pool through
  capacityClassesAttribute, capacityClassesToProto, and
  capacityClassesFromProtoPreservingNull, so the cost is small.
- Superseded: pdFromProto was a pure mapping from the API message. It
  now takes the prior model, because a list attribute needs the prior
  value to keep null (see Findings). Concurrency uses the same
  int64FromProtoPreservingNull rule as decode priority and concurrency.
- pdFromProto returns an error when the API returns pd without
  prefill_resources or prefill_autoscaling, instead of writing zero
  values into state.
- gpuCountAttribute holds the one copy of the allowed GPU counts for
  both pools. optionalUint32 replaces the explicit null guards in the
  decode request mapping so both pools use one rule.
- rolloutStartGracePeriod is now a package var so the lifecycle test
  can apply updates without a 30 s wait per step.
  SetRolloutStartGracePeriod in export_test.go sets it and restores it
  with t.Cleanup. Only sequential tests may call it; parallel tests
  resume after sequential tests finish, and go test -race passed.
- Not changed from the review: (4) prefill concurrency stays Optional
  and not Computed. The proto comment does not say the API defaults or
  inherits it, and decode concurrency has the same shape. (5) No
  plan-time check that the engine is dynamo-vllm or that prefill
  min <= max. ModifyPlan deliberately takes the engine list from the
  API instead of hardcoding it, and decode has no min <= max check
  either; the API rejects both at apply.

